---
/**
 * DoctorProfilesBlock — the `doctor_profiles` layout: the doctor bio band.
 *
 * Extracted from three live bands, not designed anew. All three are
 * `<article class="doctor doctor--feature">` — one portrait column, one bio
 * stack — and they differ only in what the stack holds:
 *
 *   src/pages/implant-dentistry/index.astro:441-473   `#doctors`, paper.
 *       Portrait + ONE `.doctor-bio.doctor-bio--lead`, two paragraphs, no rule.
 *   src/pages/cosmetic-dentistry/index.astro:369-420  `#doctors`, paper.
 *       Portrait + a lead bio, a `.doctor-rule`, then an `<aside>` for Dr. Annie
 *       carrying her own circular thumbnail beside her eyebrow.
 *   src/pages/general-dentistry/index.astro:360-400   `#doctors`, paper.
 *       Portrait (the duo photo) + TWO equal-weight `.doctor-bio` divs with a
 *       `.doctor-rule` between them. No `--lead`, no thumbnail.
 *
 * ── ONE REPEATER DRAWS ALL THREE, AND TWO FIELDS PICK THE SHAPE ──────────
 *
 * `bios[]` is the whole difference. Every row is `.doctor-bio`; two of its own
 * sub-fields decide which of the three skins it wears, and neither is a "style"
 * select an editor has to reason about:
 *
 *   `lead`  true  → `.doctor-bio--lead`, the FEATURE bio: the larger display
 *                   heading (clamp 24-32 rather than 22-28). Implant's single
 *                   bio and cosmetic's first are `--lead`; general's two are
 *                   deliberately not, because that page's SEO copy bills both
 *                   doctors equally (general-dentistry.css:741-747 says so at
 *                   length).
 *   `image` set → the SECONDARY ASIDE: an `<aside>` rather than a `<div>`, its
 *                   thumbnail inline with its eyebrow inside
 *                   `.doctor-secondary-head`, and smaller prose. A bio carrying
 *                   its own portrait is the only shape that has anywhere to put
 *                   one — `.doctor-thumb` exists nowhere else in the corpus.
 *
 * THE IMAGE WINS WHEN A ROW SETS BOTH, and that is not a coin toss: `--lead`
 * only resizes an `<h3>`, and the aside as shipped has no `<h3>` at all, so
 * honouring `lead` there would change nothing on screen while throwing away the
 * one shape that can draw the thumbnail the editor just uploaded. The row is
 * warned about rather than silently reinterpreted.
 *
 * ── THE RULE IS DERIVED FROM POSITION, NEVER STORED ──────────────────────
 *
 * `.doctor-rule` is drawn BEFORE every row but the first, which is exactly
 * where both pages that ship it put it and is the only place it can go: it is a
 * separator, so a stored "show a rule" toggle would let an editor put one above
 * the first bio (a hairline hanging under the section head) or delete the one
 * between two bios that need it. Same rule as ProcessStepsBlock's numerals and
 * MediaSplitBlock's `01/02/03` markers — position is computed, not typed.
 *
 * Its spacing is the one measured disagreement between the two sheets that draw
 * it: 32px above and below on cosmetic (cosmetic-dentistry.css:547), 28px on
 * general (general-dentistry.css:836). The signal that separates them is
 * already in the DOM — cosmetic's rule follows the FEATURE bio, general's
 * follows a plain one — so the sheet below expresses it as
 * `.doctor-bio--lead + .doctor-rule` and needs no field, no modifier class and
 * no `:has()` (which appears nowhere in this codebase). See the <style> header.
 *
 * ── THE ASIDE IS LABELLED FROM ITS OWN EYEBROW ───────────────────────────
 *
 * cosmetic-dentistry/index.astro:396 is `aria-label="Also at the practice"`, and
 * its eyebrow at :406 is the same four words. Two fields for one string is two
 * chances to drift, and the one that drifts silently is the one only screen
 * readers hear — so the eyebrow IS the accessible name here. A row with no
 * eyebrow gets no `aria-label` rather than an empty one, because
 * `aria-label=""` on a landmark is worse than none: it suppresses the element's
 * own name without supplying another.
 *
 * ── WHERE THE BAND'S OWN `body` GOES, AND WHY IT IS NOT THE BIO'S ────────
 *
 * All three templates read `{section("doctors").body}` INSIDE the feature bio,
 * not in the section head — so that paragraph's field on this layout is
 * `bios[].body`, and the preamble's `body` is the head paragraph none of the
 * three bands has. Blank on all three, and blank draws no `<p>`. Filling the
 * preamble body on a migrated band would put the doctor's prose above the
 * portrait instead of beside it, which is why the field docs below say which is
 * which.
 *
 * Every field is optional and a blank one draws NOTHING, never an empty
 * wrapper: no `.section-head` without head copy, no `.doctor--feature` without
 * either a portrait or a bio, no `.doctor-stack` for an empty repeater, no
 * `.doctor-portrait` without a picture. The band shell itself is always
 * emitted, as it is in all eight registered siblings — a block owns its band
 * (docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md 2.5), and a row that vanished entirely would take its
 * `id` with it and break whichever rail chip points at it.
 *
 * Styles are co-located per docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md 2.5, modelled on FinalBand.astro
 * and on TechGridBlock, its sibling in this wave: no page namespace, `--vs-*`
 * tokens directly, band as a prop, self-owned padding, zero margin.
 *
 * Spec: docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md Phase 4, Wave A. Nothing imports this file yet —
 * registry.ts, manifest.ts and vs-content-model.php are a later commit.
 */
import { Image } from "astro:assets";

/** The layout's image sub-field, as wpgraphql-acf returns an ACF image.
    Same shape as MediaSplitBlock.astro:125-132 and TechGridBlock.astro:57-64,
    and for the same reason: the manifest selects
    `image { node { sourceUrl altText mediaDetails { width height } } }`. */
export interface DoctorProfilesImage {
  node?: {
    sourceUrl?: string | null;
    altText?: string | null;
    mediaDetails?: { width?: number | null; height?: number | null } | null;
  } | null;
}

/**
 * One row of the layout's `bios` repeater — GraphQL type
 * `PageFieldsBlocksBios`. Every sub-field is optional at the schema level
 * because ACF returns null for anything an editor never filled in; typing any
 * of them as a plain string would lie to the filter below.
 */
export interface DoctorProfilesBio {
  /** The name line — "Dr. Bryce Richardson, DDS". Doubles as the aside's
      accessible name; see the header. */
  eyebrow?: string | null;
  /** May contain `<em>`, and nothing else (docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md, Fact 3) —
      "Top of his class — <em>and his hometown.</em>". Hence `set:html`. */
  heading?: string | null;
  body?: string | null;
  /** The second paragraph. Only implant's bio ships one
      (implant-dentistry/index.astro:466-470); blank draws no `<p>`. */
  body2?: string | null;
  /** Marks the FEATURE bio — the larger heading. See the header for what
      happens when a row sets this and uploads a picture too. */
  lead?: boolean | null;
  /** The bio's OWN portrait — the circular `.doctor-thumb`. Its presence is
      what makes this row the secondary aside. Not to be confused with the
      band's `image`, which is the tall portrait beside the whole stack. */
  image?: DoctorProfilesImage | null;
  imageAlt?: string | null;
}

interface Props {
  /** DOM id, already deduplicated by PageBlocks.astro. Absent when blank. */
  id?: string;
  /** The same value as `id`. Accepted so the spread in PageBlocks is lossless. */
  anchor?: string | null;
  /** Rail label. Read by the page that builds the rail, not by this band. */
  navLabel?: string | null;
  band?: string | null;
  eyebrow?: string | null;
  /** May contain `<em>`, and nothing else (docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md, Fact 3). */
  heading?: string | null;
  /** The SECTION HEAD's paragraph, which none of the three live bands has. The
      doctor's prose is `bios[].body` — see the header. Blank draws no `<p>`. */
  body?: string | null;
  /** The glass chip on the portrait — "Founder · Surgical &amp; Restorative",
      "Dr. Bryce &amp; Dr. Annie". Absolutely positioned inside
      `.doctor-portrait`, so it is drawn only when there is a portrait. */
  pill?: string | null;
  /** The band's tall 4:5 portrait. */
  image?: DoctorProfilesImage | null;
  imageAlt?: string | null;
  bios?: (DoctorProfilesBio | null)[] | null;
}

/**
 * band value -> the modifier class the page's markup already uses.
 *
 * Read this map, not an ancestor class. `.section.alt` is charcoal green on all
 * three hub sheets but cream on teeth-whitening.css:237 (Fact 4), so a block
 * that inherited its band would be wrong on some page the moment a section is
 * dragged. `paper` maps to nothing because a paper band is a bare
 * `class="section"` in every template — which is what all three doctor bands
 * are.
 */
const BAND_CLASS: Record<string, string | undefined> = {
  paper: undefined,
  cream: "cream",
  "sage-pale": "dark",
  sage: "sage",
  charcoal: "alt",
};

const {
  id,
  /* `paper` and not something louder: it is what the PHP field stores as its
     own default_value (vs-content-model.php, block_preamble()), so a row an
     editor has never opened renders the band wp-admin is showing them. The
     branch is unreachable for any saved row — ACF returns the stored choice —
     which is exactly why it must not invent a different one. */
  band = "paper",
  eyebrow,
  heading,
  body,
  pill,
  image,
  imageAlt,
  bios,
} = Astro.props;

const bandKey = typeof band === "string" && band !== "" ? band : "paper";
const bandClass = BAND_CLASS[bandKey];

const text = (input: unknown): string =>
  typeof input === "string" ? input.trim() : "";

/**
 * The eyebrow carries `light` on the two bands the templates spell
 * `.section.dark` / `.section.sage`. It is INERT under the cascade below
 * (`.section.dark .section-head .eyebrow` already outranks `.eyebrow.light`);
 * it is reproduced because this phase is verified by an HTML diff, not by a
 * rendering. Same rule and same wording as TechGridBlock.astro:148-157 and
 * ProcessStepsBlock.astro:143-152.
 */
const eyebrowLight = bandKey === "sage-pale" || bandKey === "sage";

const eyebrowText = text(eyebrow);
const headingHtml = text(heading);
const bodyText = text(body);
const hasHead = eyebrowText !== "" || headingHtml !== "" || bodyText !== "";

/**
 * The band's portrait, or nothing.
 *
 * `<Image>` refuses a remote source without explicit intrinsic dimensions, so a
 * media item WordPress recorded no size for cannot be rendered at all — an SVG,
 * or an upload that did not finish. That degrades to a bio-only band rather
 * than failing a build an editor triggered (the rule MediaSplitBlock.astro:485-506
 * and TechGridBlock.astro:196-206 both run on), and the omission is reported to
 * the build log where an engineer will see it. All three live rows carry a
 * 1200x1800 WebP and none takes this path.
 */
const node = image?.node ?? null;
const src = typeof node?.sourceUrl === "string" ? node.sourceUrl : "";
const width = node?.mediaDetails?.width ?? 0;
const height = node?.mediaDetails?.height ?? 0;
const hasPortrait = src !== "" && width > 0 && height > 0;

/** Editor override first, then the alt text stored on the file itself. */
const alt = text(imageAlt) || text(node?.altText) || "";

/* The pill is `position: absolute` inside `.doctor-portrait`
   (cosmetic-dentistry.css:499-508). With no portrait there is no positioned
   box to hold it, and a stray chip would land wherever the nearest positioned
   ancestor happens to be — so it is dropped, loudly. */
const pillText = text(pill);
if (pillText !== "" && !hasPortrait) {
  console.warn(
    `[blocks] doctor_profiles on ${Astro.url.pathname}${id ? `#${id}` : ""} has a portrait pill but no portrait — ` +
      "the pill sits inside the photo, so it is not drawn. Add the band image in wp-admin.",
  );
}

if (src !== "" && !hasPortrait) {
  console.warn(
    `[blocks] doctor_profiles on ${Astro.url.pathname}${id ? `#${id}` : ""} has a band portrait with no recorded ` +
      "width/height — drawing the bios without it",
  );
}

/**
 * The bios, trimmed, shaped and emptied out.
 *
 * `Array.isArray` first because an unqueried repeater arrives `undefined` and a
 * repeater an editor never opened arrives `null`, and `.map` on either throws
 * during the build of whatever page the band happens to sit on.
 *
 * The filter drops a row only when EVERY text field is blank — the empty row
 * ACF leaves behind when someone clicks "Add row" and changes their mind. It is
 * deliberately not "a bio needs a heading": a row saved with only a name line
 * is a row an editor meant to save, and dropping it here would delete published
 * copy with no error anywhere to explain it. It runs BEFORE the divider is
 * derived, which is the whole point of doing it here — numbering the rows first
 * would spend the first position on a row that draws nothing and hang a
 * hairline under the section head.
 *
 * A row whose only content is an uploaded picture is kept: the thumbnail IS
 * content, and an aside holding just a face is a shape an editor can see and
 * fix. A row with nothing at all cannot be.
 */
const rows = (Array.isArray(bios) ? bios : [])
  .map((row) => {
    const rowNode = row?.image?.node ?? null;
    const rowSrc = typeof rowNode?.sourceUrl === "string" ? rowNode.sourceUrl : "";
    const rowWidth = rowNode?.mediaDetails?.width ?? 0;
    const rowHeight = rowNode?.mediaDetails?.height ?? 0;
    return {
      eyebrow: text(row?.eyebrow),
      heading: text(row?.heading),
      body: text(row?.body),
      body2: text(row?.body2),
      /* `=== true` because ACF returns null for a true/false an editor never
         touched, and null must mean "not the feature bio", not "maybe". */
      lead: row?.lead === true,
      /* The IMAGE FIELD, not a renderable one, is the shape switch — a row
         whose upload lost its dimensions is still the aside its editor meant,
         drawn without the thumbnail rather than demoted to a plain bio, which
         would also silently drop its `aria-label`. */
      secondary: rowSrc !== "",
      src: rowSrc,
      width: rowWidth,
      height: rowHeight,
      hasThumb: rowSrc !== "" && rowWidth > 0 && rowHeight > 0,
      alt: text(row?.imageAlt) || text(rowNode?.altText) || "",
    };
  })
  .filter(
    (row) =>
      row.eyebrow !== "" ||
      row.heading !== "" ||
      row.body !== "" ||
      row.body2 !== "" ||
      row.secondary,
  );

for (const row of rows) {
  if (row.secondary && !row.hasThumb) {
    console.warn(
      `[blocks] doctor_profiles bio "${row.eyebrow || "(unnamed)"}" on ${Astro.url.pathname}${id ? `#${id}` : ""} ` +
        "has a thumbnail with no recorded width/height — drawing the aside without it",
    );
  }
  if (row.secondary && row.lead) {
    console.warn(
      `[blocks] doctor_profiles bio "${row.eyebrow || "(unnamed)"}" on ${Astro.url.pathname}${id ? `#${id}` : ""} ` +
        "is marked as the feature bio AND carries its own photo — drawing the photo, which the feature shape has " +
        "nowhere to put. Clear one of the two in wp-admin.",
    );
  }
}

/* No portrait and no bios means no article: `.doctor--feature` is a two-track
   grid with 60px of gap, so an empty one is a silent 60px hole in the band. */
const hasArticle = hasPortrait || rows.length > 0;
---

{/* `data-vs-reveal-batch` on the section, exactly as all three templates carry
    it. animations.js:531 animates the section's DIRECT children, which is the
    one `.wrap` — so this is a single fade of the whole band, not a per-bio
    stagger. Moving it to `.doctor--feature` would be a different animation. */}
<section class:list={["section", bandClass]} id={id} data-vs-reveal-batch>
  <div class="wrap">
    {
      hasHead && (
        <div class="section-head">
          {eyebrowText && (
            <span class:list={["eyebrow", eyebrowLight && "light"]}>{eyebrowText}</span>
          )}
          {headingHtml && (
            <h2>
              <Fragment set:html={headingHtml} />
            </h2>
          )}
          {bodyText && <p>{bodyText}</p>}
        </div>
      )
    }

    {
      hasArticle && (
        <article class="doctor doctor--feature">
          {/* The pill is NOT flushed against the <Image>, unlike the seams in
              the stack below, and this comment sits OUTSIDE the portrait for
              the reason TechGridBlock.astro:277-280 gives: inside it, Astro
              emits the newlines around it as a text node the templates do not
              have. The pill itself can keep its own line — `.doctor-portrait`
              is a block box whose only other child is an `img` set to
              `display: block`, so the whitespace a blank pill leaves behind
              cannot open a line box and cannot render, and the templates put
              the two on their own lines too
              (cosmetic-dentistry/index.astro:377-386). */}
          {hasPortrait && (
            <div class="doctor-portrait">
              {pillText && <span class="doctor-pill">{pillText}</span>}
              <Image
                src={src}
                width={width}
                height={height}
                alt={alt}
                {/* A SUPERSET OF ALL THREE HUBS, ON PURPOSE. general ships
                    [420,640,880,1100]; cosmetic and implant ship [420,640,880]
                    (general-dentistry/index.astro:373,
                    cosmetic-dentistry/index.astro:382,
                    implant-dentistry/index.astro:454). Carrying the union costs
                    the two shorter pages one extra generated candidate and can
                    never degrade them — the browser picks from what it is
                    offered — whereas trimming to the shorter list would take a
                    real candidate away from general on wide screens. The
                    emitted srcset therefore differs from two templates by one
                    entry, which is a byte difference and not a visual one. */}
                widths={[420, 640, 880, 1100]}
                sizes="(max-width: 991px) 100vw, 50vw"
              />
            </div>
          )}

          {rows.length > 0 && (
            <div class="doctor-stack">
              {rows.map((row, i) => (
                <Fragment>{/* SEAM 1 — the divider, `}{` flush against the bio
                    that follows it. It is drawn BEFORE every row but the
                    first, which is the only place a separator can go and is
                    where both pages that ship it put it
                    (cosmetic-dentistry/index.astro:394,
                    general-dentistry/index.astro:385). On its own line the
                    false branch — the first bio, i.e. every band that has only
                    one — would carry a leading whitespace text node into
                    `.doctor-stack` that none of the three templates has. The
                    comment lives INSIDE the expression because a comment node
                    on its own line would reintroduce exactly what it
                    documents. */
                  i > 0 && <div class="doctor-rule" aria-hidden="true" />
                }{
                  row.secondary ? (
                    <aside
                      class="doctor-bio doctor-bio--secondary"
                      aria-label={row.eyebrow || undefined}
                    >
                      <div class="doctor-secondary-head">
                        {row.hasThumb && (
                          <div class="doctor-thumb">
                            <Image
                              src={row.src}
                              width={row.width}
                              height={row.height}
                              alt={row.alt}
                              widths={[120, 200]}
                              sizes="(max-width: 991px) 48px, 56px"
                            />
                          </div>
                        )}
                        {row.eyebrow && <span class="eyebrow">{row.eyebrow}</span>}
                      </div>{/* SEAM 2 — `}{` flush, nothing between. No live
                          aside carries a heading (cosmetic's is head + prose),
                          so this branch is false on the one row that ships this
                          shape; on its own line it would leave a whitespace
                          text node inside the aside that the template does not
                          have, on the band this block exists to reproduce
                          byte-for-byte. The comment lives INSIDE the expression
                          because a comment node on its own line would
                          reintroduce exactly what it documents. */
                        row.heading && (
                          <h3>
                            <Fragment set:html={row.heading} />
                          </h3>
                        )
                      }
                      {row.body && <p>{row.body}</p>}{/* SEAM 3 — flush. Four of
                          the five live bios have one paragraph and no `body_2`,
                          so an own-line expression here would add a second
                          whitespace node to every one of them. */
                        row.body2 && <p>{row.body2}</p>
                      }
                    </aside>
                  ) : (
                    <div class:list={["doctor-bio", row.lead && "doctor-bio--lead"]}>
                      {row.eyebrow && <span class="eyebrow">{row.eyebrow}</span>}
                      {row.heading && (
                        <h3>
                          <Fragment set:html={row.heading} />
                        </h3>
                      )}
                      {row.body && <p>{row.body}</p>}{/* SEAM 4 — flush, same
                          reason as seam 3: `body_2` is filled on exactly one
                          bio in the corpus (implant's), so the false branch is
                          the common case and it must cost nothing. */
                        row.body2 && <p>{row.body2}</p>
                      }
                    </div>
                  )
                }</Fragment>
              ))}
            </div>
          )}
        </article>
      )
    }
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* ══ WHERE THESE RULES COME FROM ═══════════════════════════════════════════
     Three source sheets, because this block draws three live bands:

       src/styles/pages/implant-dentistry.css   `.impd`  — one lead bio
       src/styles/pages/cosmetic-dentistry.css  `.cdent` — lead + aside
       src/styles/pages/general-dentistry.css   `.gdent` — two equal bios

     Each page's local `--sage` / `--ink` / `--line` / `--r-lg` / `--shadow-lg`
     aliases are resolved back to the `--vs-*` tokens they alias one-for-one
     (cosmetic-dentistry.css:34-55, and the identical tables at the head of the
     other two). Rendered values are unchanged.

     The three sheets agree declaration-for-declaration on the portrait, the
     pill, the stack and the eyebrow. They disagree in exactly three places, and
     every one of the three is a real, measured difference between live pages
     rather than drift — so each is reproduced from a signal already in the DOM,
     never flattened to whichever page was read last:

       1. HEADING SIZE. `.doctor-bio--lead h3` is clamp(24px, 2.6vw, 32px)
          (cosmetic:523-529, and implant:1014-1023 where the only bio IS the
          lead); a plain `.doctor-bio h3` is clamp(22px, 2.3vw, 28px)
          (general:809-818). Keyed on `--lead`, which is what the two pages
          differ by.
       2. RULE SPACING. 32px on cosmetic (:547), 28px on general (:836); 28px
          and 24px respectively at the 1100px breakpoint (:932, :1276-1278). The
          cosmetic rule is the one that follows the FEATURE bio, so it is keyed
          on `.doctor-bio--lead + .doctor-rule` — an adjacent sibling, no
          `:has()` (which appears nowhere in this codebase) and no modifier
          class this block would have to invent and then diff against.
       3. PARAGRAPH SPACING. Implant's bio has two paragraphs and spaces them
          `margin: 0 0 14px` with `p:last-child` clearing it (:1029-1038);
          cosmetic and general have one paragraph each and write `margin: 0`
          (:533-537, :824-830). Implant's pair is the general case — for a
          single paragraph the `:last-child` rule resolves to exactly
          `margin: 0` — so it is the one written here, and all three bands
          render identically.

     ── AND WHY EVERY RULE IS QUALIFIED WITH `.section` ─────────────────────
     Astro's attribute scoping compiles `.doctor-portrait` to
     `.doctor-portrait[cid]` — specificity (0,2,0), which TIES with the page
     sheets' own `.cdent .doctor-portrait`, and a tie is decided by bundle
     order. The house rule is never to build on that tie, so every rule below is
     written `.section .thing` → `.section[cid] .thing[cid]` at (0,4,0), which
     clears all three page sheets outright. Nothing here depends on which
     stylesheet a bundler happens to emit first.

     The handful of intra-file overrides (`--lead` over the base heading,
     `--secondary` over the base prose) are ties WITHIN this file, decided by
     source order, which is a language guarantee rather than a build artefact —
     the page sheets settle the same pairs the same way. Each is written
     directly below the rule it overrides. */

  /* ---------- band shell ----------
     `.section` owns its padding and has no margin at all, so this band cannot
     collapse into whichever band an editor drags above or below it. Same rule
     as FinalBand.astro and every sibling block.
     padding: implant-dentistry.css:307 = cosmetic-dentistry.css:199 =
     general-dentistry.css:325-327. `cream` and `sage` are in none of the three
     hub sheets — no hub places those bands — so their values come from
     tokens.css via the block family's shell (FaqBlock.astro:235-250), which is
     the contract the band select promises. */
  .section {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 110px 0;
    background: var(--vs-paper);
  }
  .section.cream {
    background: var(--vs-cream);
  }
  /* cosmetic-dentistry.css:216 / implant-dentistry.css:333-335 /
     general-dentistry.css:351-353 */
  .section.dark {
    background: var(--vs-sage-pale);
  }
  .section.sage {
    background: var(--vs-sage);
  }
  /* cosmetic-dentistry.css:200 / implant-dentistry.css:310-312 /
     general-dentistry.css:328-331 */
  .section.alt {
    background: var(--vs-charcoal-green);
  }

  /* Keep the heading clear of the stacked global Nav + sticky TOC when a rail
     chip is clicked. implant-dentistry.css:301-303,
     cosmetic-dentistry.css:195-197, general-dentistry.css:320-322 (all written
     there as `section[id]`). Both custom properties are set on the page wrapper
     and inherit down; the fallbacks are the desktop measurements those sheets
     declare (cosmetic-dentistry.css:35-36). */
  .section[id] {
    scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--nav-h, 77px) + var(--toc-h, 68px) + 12px);
  }

  /* cosmetic-dentistry.css:58 = implant-dentistry.css:48-52 =
     general-dentistry.css:66-70 */
  .wrap {
    max-width: 1240px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 40px;
  }

  /* cosmetic-dentistry.css:60-64 = implant-dentistry.css:54-61 =
     general-dentistry.css:72-79 */
  .eyebrow {
